A||N||10||Earne||High Idle||||5||
Z||000000||Earne||03-05-2000||02:17 PM||||Hope someone is on this board today.  With near 70 degrees and sunny, I know I wouldn't be if I didn't have a problem.  I had some engine pieces chromed (most of it actually) over the winter, put it back together today, and I can't get the idle down.  I installed a new TPS and IAC while everything was apart.  When I started it the engine immediately jumped up to about 2500 RPM.  Any ideas?  86 1/2 GT, Auto.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||03-05-2000||02:36 PM||||The idle will drop a little when you put it in gear.  Drive around for 30 or 40 miles.  I'll bet you had the battery disconnected when you were doing the motor.  This will cause the computer to need to reset itself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||b_b_a_d||03-05-2000||03:21 PM||||I agree with monkeyman on the computer resetting itself if the battery was disconnected but 2500 rpm is pretty high.<P>I would consider taking the new TPS off and having it checked just to make sure it is working properly not to mention checking all the related electrical connections.  <P>I only suggest this since it requires no out of pocket expense...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by b_b_a_d (edited 03-05-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-05-2000||03:33 PM||||UUUm i reset my computer on almost a daily basis! I haven't run in to any idle issues except when i tried to replace the vacuum hoses myself... I've found if you don't zip tie them they may come loose. I'd go fishing for a vacuum leak first.. what did you have chromed? the Pelenum (sp) you sure you hooked up all the vacuum lines? Did you have the distributor out or loose? did you bump the timing? have you replaced your ICM (ignition conrol module) and not used the dielectric grease? did you mess with your idle settings inadvertantly upping the idle?... just more things to add to your plate...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||03-05-2000||03:59 PM||||You mentioned that the IAC was one of the components you changed.  That being the case, it's almost certain that computer needs some time to recalibrate itself. I would take Monkeymans advice and drive the car for a while at speeds above 40 mph.  This allows the computer to determine the starting point for the idle air valve.  Unless you have a vacuum leak, this should help.  ||||reg||7
Z||000005||fiero56||03-05-2000||04:10 PM||||Problem lies in the fact the IAC was replaced. The pintle needs to be able to seat properly, everyone was correct, drive around town for a little bit, try to drive 35+ for a few minutes. All should be fine afterwards.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Earne||03-06-2000||12:18 AM||||Thanks guys, I believe the computer reset and the IAC seating, was the problem.  Just in case, I did remove the plenum again and checked all the vacuum lines.  Yes the battery was disconnected.  Chromed a lot, plenum, throttle body, tb coolant lines, crossover heat sheild, trans dip stick handle and tube, rear heat shield, cruise hardware bracket, dogbone brackets, oil dip stick handle, grill mounting brackets, deck lid hindges and custom shock tower covers.  Now it goes to Indianna to install a brand new set of T-Tops.  Yea, I know, way over the edge.  Anyway, the Toy is running great now, thanks again.  If anyone cares, I'll post a couple pics.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||03-06-2000||05:04 AM||||I care. Post pics.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Earne||03-06-2000||06:55 AM||||Monkeyman, I thought I got up early.  I'll post them later today, first I have to take them, thanks.||||reg||1
